        // About
        section#about.about-section.text-center
            .container.px-4.px-lg-5
                h1.text-white About Me
                .row.gx-4.gx-lg-5.align-items-center
                    .col-xl-5
                        img.img-fluid.mb-0.mb-lg-0(src='assets/img/headshot.jpg', alt='Lennart J. Schulze')
                    .col-xl-7
                        .text-justified.text-lg-left
                            p.text-white.mb-3
                                | Hey! I am Lennart, I am a mechanical engineer by training and a computational scientist by practice. I have recently finished my Ph.D. thesis at the TU Dresden and the Max-Planck Institute of Molecular Cell Biology and Genetrics and am transitioning into industry. The past four years, I have been part of the group of 
                                a(href='https://www.mpi-cbg.de/research/researchgroups/currentgroups/ivo-sbalzarini/group-leader') Ivo Sbalzarini
                                | that focuses on scientific machine learning and numerical simulations. My research here has focused on the description and adaptive discretization of dynamic surfaces, which are ubiquitous in nature and engineering.
                                | Before, I have studied Mechanical Engineering for my bachelor's and master's degrees at the TU München, with stays in Tokyo and as a visiting researcher at the Northwestern University, where I have been working on the simulation of water waves in the group of Robert Dalrymple.
                                br
                                br
                                | My work sits right at the intersection of physics, numerical methods and high-performance computing.
                                br
                                br
                                | You can reach me at 
                                a.email-link(data-u1='lennart', data-u2='jschulze', data-d1='gmail', data-d2='com', href='#')
                                    span.email-text reach me by email
                                | .

        // Projects
        section#projects.projects-section
            .container.px-3.px-lg-3
                .row.gx-4.gx-lg-5.justify-content-center
                    .col-lg-8
                        h1.text-black.mb-2 Research
                // Featured Project Row
                .row.gx-0.mb-4.align-items-center
                    h1.text-white.m-0.p-0(style='line-height:0; height:0; overflow:hidden;') Research
                    .col-12
                        // Top: video left + text right; second video tucked under the text
                        .row.gx-0(style='align-items: flex-start;')
                            .col-md-7
                                .overflow-hidden
                                    video.img-fluid(src='assets/img/thesis_valpha.mp4', autoplay='', loop='', muted='', playsinline='', alt='Morphogenetic simulation alpha', style='clip-path: inset(0 13% 0 0); margin-right: -13%; width: 100%;')
                            .col-md-5
                                .featured-text.text-justified.text-lg-left.p-4
                                    h4.text-black Simulating morphogenetic models
                                    p.text-black.mb-0
                                        | Self-organizing shapes emerge from the interaction of physics and geometry. Here, we solve the non-linear and second-order reaction-diffusion equations within the continuously deforming surface, and allow for local growth depending on the concentration of the surface chemical. The PDE and the observed Turing patterns in the surface are influenced by the local emerging curvature, and the local curvature is determined by the PDE dynamics in the surface: A full feedback loop, from which organic shapes arise.
                                        br
                                        br
                                        | Manuscript in preparation.
                                .overflow-hidden(style='width: 140%; margin-left: -40%; margin-top: 1rem;')
                                    video.img-fluid(src='assets/img/thesis_vgamma.mp4', autoplay='', loop='', muted='', playsinline='', alt='Morphogenetic simulation gamma', style='clip-path: inset(10% 13% 10% 10%); width: 100%;')

                // Project One Row
                .row.gx-0.mb-4.align-items-center(style='margin-top: -9rem;')
                    .col-12
                        // Project description
                        .row.gx-0
                            .col-md-8.mx-auto
                                .featured-text.text-justified.text-lg-left.p-4
                                    h4.text-black Adaptive sampling of surfaces
                                    p.text-black.mb-0
                                        | Sampling curved surfaces with local regularity and global adaptivity is a long-standing problem in computer graphics and numerical methods. In this work, we use a gradient descent constrained to the curved surface to regularize point distributions for analytical surfaces and obtained from 3D scanning devices such as the 3D Stanford bunny. 
                                        br
                                        | The computed point discretizations of the curved surface allow for straightforward rendering without artifacts and robustness and accuracy of numerical methods during simulations.
                                        br
                                        br
                                        | Currently under revision. Preprint is available at 
                                        a(href='https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.03803') arXiv:2605.03803
                                        | .
                        // Two small images side by side, centered, underneath
                        .row.gx-3.justify-content-center.align-items-center.mt-3
                            .col-md-3.col-6.text-center
                                img.img-fluid(src='assets/img/cow.svg', alt='...', style='width: 150%; max-width: none;')
                            .col-md-3.col-6.text-center
                                img.img-fluid(src='assets/img/bunny.svg', alt='...', style='width: 130%; max-width: none;')

                .row.gx-0.mb-4.align-items-center
                    .col-12
                        // Full-width black background wrapper
                        div(style='background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff 0%, #000 8%, #000 92%, #fff 100%); width: 100vw; margin-left: calc(50% - 50vw); padding: 3rem 0;')
                            .container
                                // Project description (white text on black)
                                .row.gx-0
                                    .col-md-8.mx-auto
                                        .featured-text.text-justified.text-lg-left.pt-4.px-4.pb-0
                                            h4.text-white Describing and quantifying dynamic surfaces in numerical simulations
                                            p.text-white.mb-0
                                                | Real-world problems involve complex, and often dynamic surfaces. Either as boundary conditions, or as the domain of interest. An illustrative example of a dynamic surface is a hydrodynamic, incompressible droplet under surface tension, governed by the multi-phase Navier-Stokes equations.
                                                br
                                                | To track the surface, we propose a method based on local high-order polynomial regression, enabling the computation of derivative geometric quantities such as surface normals and mean and Gaussian curvatures.
                                                br
                                                br
                                                | The paper is available at the
                                                a(href='https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcp.2024.113262') Journal of Computational Physics
                                                | .
                                // Video underneath
                                .row.gx-0.justify-content-center
                                    .col-md-8
                                        video.img-fluid(src='assets/img/splittingDroplet.mp4', autoplay='', loop='', muted='', playsinline='', alt='Splitting droplet simulation')

                .row.gx-0.mb-0.align-items-center
                    .col-12
                        // Project description
                        .row.gx-0
                            .col-md-8.mx-auto
                                .featured-text.text-justified.text-lg-left.pt-4.px-4.pb-0
                                    h4.text-black Saving computational resources in water wave simulations
                                    p.text-black.mb-0
                                        | Adaptive resolution methods that place more computational nodes where required are a classic approach to reduce computational effort and accordingly speed up simulations, while retaining high accuracy. In this work, we proposed a different approach: While the point discretization of the domain of interest is homogeneous, we use a higher-order variant of Smoothed Particle Hydrodynamics (SPH) in regions with demand for high accuracy, and a standard, more cost-efficient variant in the other regions. Especially for deep water waves, this enables a much faster simulation of water wave propagation.
                                        br
                                        br
                                        | This work was presented at the SPHERIC 2022 International Workshop and its proceedings paper is available at 
                                        a(href='https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.10064') arXiv:2511.10064
                                        | .
                        // Image lower right (flush with text right edge)
                        .row.gx-0
                            .col-md-5
                            .col-md-5.col-8
                                img.img-fluid.mb-0(src='assets/img/waterWave.svg', alt='Water wave simulation')

